<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diogenes the Cynic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 412–<a href="/years/323-bce">323 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Diogenes of Sinope</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diogenes the Cynic (c. 412–323 BCE) was a Greek philosopher born in Sinope on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and the best-known figure of the Cynic school. Exiled from his home city, he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Corinth in deliberate poverty, reportedly sleeping in a large jar, and mocked social convention to show that virtue needed only self-sufficiency. Anecdotes about him, including a meeting with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, were collected by Diogenes Laertius.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diogenes-the-cynic.jpg" alt="Diogenes the Cynic" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diogenes_Mosaic_R%C3%B6misch-Germanisches_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diogenes was born in Sinope, a Milesian colony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, the son of Hicesias, a money-changer. He and his father were accused of debasing the currency. Ancient sources disagree on who was responsible, and modern scholars are divided: some see the episode as a fiction symbolising his aim to revalue the currency, meaning to challenge conventional values, while others point to debased Sinopean coins as evidence of a historical core. It is unclear whether he was banished or fled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> notes that the hardships of exile made him a philosopher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Diogenes advocated a shameless life lived according to nature rather than social convention. He lived by begging, sheltered in a large ceramic storage jar, and was said to wander the marketplace by day with a lit lamp, saying that he was looking for a man, that is, a wise man. Asked where he came from, he answered kosmopolitēs, a citizen of the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> is said to have called him <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socrates">Socrates</a> gone mad.</p><p>According to Diogenes Laertius, he wrote dialogues, letters and tragedies, none of which survive. The best-known anecdote has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, probably in 336 BCE at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>, ask whether he wanted anything; Diogenes replied that Alexander should stand a little out of his sun, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> reports Alexander as saying that if he were not Alexander he would be Diogenes.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ancient sources give several accounts of his death, among them self-asphyxiation, a raw octopus and an infected dog bite. A story that he died on the same night as Alexander is probably legend, and one modern scholar thinks he most plausibly died of old age, in or near Corinth. The Corinthians arranged his funeral and buried him outside the city walls. After his death Cynicism diverged: one branch, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeno-of-citium">Zeno of Citium</a>, evolved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stoicism">Stoicism</a>, while another, beginning with Crates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a>, preserved his principles until Sallustius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Emesa</a> in the 5th century CE. His attitude to the rich gave the word cynicism its modern sense.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diogenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Diogenes the Cynic</b> was born there c. 412 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/sinope">Sinope</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sinope, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Ancient Corinth, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.03,35.14&amp;year=-355" aria-label="Sinope on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Diogenes the Cynic"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5326 3972h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5326 3972h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.03,35.14&amp;year=-355">What was here in 355 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Stand out of my light.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laërtius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 6 &#183; tr. R. D. Hicks, 1925 &#183; his answer to Alexander the Great, as reported by Diogenes Laërtius &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Lives_of_the_Eminent_Philosophers/Book_VI" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am a citizen of the world.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laërtius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 6 &#183; tr. R. D. Hicks, 1925 &#183; asked where he came from, as reported by Diogenes Laërtius &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Lives_of_the_Eminent_Philosophers/Book_VI" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;This at least, if nothing else – to be prepared for every fortune.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laërtius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 6 &#183; tr. R. D. Hicks, 1925 &#183; asked what he had gained from philosophy, as reported by Diogenes Laërtius &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Lives_of_the_Eminent_Philosophers/Book_VI" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I am looking for a man.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 6 &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1853 &#183; what he said, carrying a lit lamp about in the daytime &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57342/pg57342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…be an absurd thing if Agesilaus and Epaminondas are to live in the mud, and some miserable wretches, who have been initiated, are to be in the islands of the blest.&#8221;</p><footer>Diogenes Laertius, Lives of Eminent Philosophers, book 6 &#183; tr. C. D. Yonge, 1853 &#183; his reply to the Athenians who urged him to be initiated into the Mysteries, which promised the initiated the best places after death &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57342/pg57342-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
